# --- Real CI caught a third copy of the bug, missed by the sandbox checks ----

# First push's `validate-pr` run (after allocating VTID-03767) surfaced this
# from the real Gateway Jest job:
#   FAIL test/autopilot-onboarding-seed-bundle.test.ts
#   day0 template onboarding_maxina matches the seeded row field-for-field
#   Expected: "Say hello to Vitana"
#   Received: "Say hello to Maxina"

grep -n "Say hello to Maxina\|onboarding_maxina" supabase/migrations/*.sql
#   -> supabase/migrations/20260607000000_BOOTSTRAP_seed_community_onboarding_autopilot.sql:97
#   -> seed_community_onboarding_autopilot(): a Postgres function fired by an
#      AFTER INSERT trigger on public.user_tenants for EVERY new community
#      member — hardcodes the day0 onboarding bundle, including the wrong
#      "Say hello to Maxina" / "Your AI companion Maxina..." row. This one
#      was live in production, not just test-side content.

# Never edit an already-applied migration file in place. New migration
# instead, CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION with only the two text values fixed:

diff <(sed -n '/VALUES/,/^  )/p' supabase/migrations/20260607000000_BOOTSTRAP_seed_community_onboarding_autopilot.sql) \
     <(sed -n '/VALUES/,/^  )/p' supabase/migrations/20260827090000_vtid_03767_fix_seed_community_onboarding_autopilot.sql)
#   -> Output: outputs/seed-migration-diff.txt — single-line diff, every
#      other row/field byte-for-byte identical.

# The test's own file-finder only picks ONE file by suffix match via raw
# readdirSync (order not guaranteed / not chronological) — unsafe now that a
# second, superseding migration exists. Fixed loadSeedMigration() to sort
# matches and read the LAST (chronologically latest, since migrations are
# timestamp-prefixed) one. New migration filename kept ending in
# `_seed_community_onboarding_autopilot.sql` on purpose so the (now-fixed)
# finder's suffix filter still matches it.

python3 -c "
import re
sql = open('../../supabase/migrations/20260827090000_vtid_03767_fix_seed_community_onboarding_autopilot.sql', encoding='utf-8').read()
pattern = re.compile(r\"\('(onboarding_[a-z0-9_]+)',\s*'((?:[^']|'')*)',\s*'((?:[^']|'')*)',\s*'([a-z]+)',\s*'([a-z]+)',\s*(\d+),\s*(\d+),\s*(\d+)\)\")
rows = {m.group(1): m.groups() for m in pattern.finditer(sql)}
print('onboarding_maxina row:', rows.get('onboarding_maxina'))
print('row count:', len(rows))
"
#   -> Output: outputs/seed-migration-parse.txt
#   -> onboarding_maxina row: ('onboarding_maxina', 'Say hello to Vitana',
#      'Your AI companion Vitana is ready to get to know you. Start a
#      conversation!', 'community', 'medium', '7', '1', '60')
#   -> row count: 8 (unchanged from before — no row added/removed)

# Same regex the test itself uses, re-run by hand against the new file to
# confirm the parse matches STAGE_TEMPLATES.day0's onboarding_maxina entry
# (title: 'Say hello to Vitana', summary: 'Your AI companion Vitana is
# ready to get to know you. Start a conversation!', domain: community,
# priority: medium, impact_score: 7, effort_score: 1,
# time_estimate_seconds: 60 — exact match, confirmed by inspection).

cd services/gateway && npx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json
#   -> clean (same pre-existing warning only) after the test file edit, too.
